#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124286 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7ae919f-4a7f-50e3-8c2f-7cea0b5a66ee" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Second Solution to a Differential Equation Using Reduction of Order"}] :question/id #uuid "2230e2dd-e47a-5db3-bb07-f89d0396440b" :question/math-academy-id "e-6573" :question/problem "Given that $y_1(t) = e^{-t}$ is a solution to the equation $y'' - 3y ' - 4y = 0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution $y_2(t)$ to the equation." :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let $y_{2} = u(t)e^{-t}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & =u{e}^{-t} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{e}^{-t}-u{e}^{-t} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{e}^{-t}-2{u}^{′}{e}^{-t}+u{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}-3{y}^{′}-4y & =0 \\\\ ({u}^{″}{e}^{-t}-2{u}^{′}{e}^{-t}+u{e}^{-t})-3({u}^{′}{e}^{-t}-u{e}^{-t})-4u{e}^{-t} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{e}^{-t}-5{u}^{′}{e}^{-t} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{-t}({u}^{″}-5{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $e^t \\neq 0,$ we must have\n\n$$\nu'' - 5u' = 0.\n$$\n\nLet $w = u'.$ Then our equation becomes a first-order equation,\n\n$$\nw' - 5w=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw = c_1 e^{5t}.\n$$\n\nNow, since since $w = u',$ we have\n\n$$\nu' = c_1e^{5t} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad u = \\dfrac{c_1}{5} e^{5t} + c_2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for $y_2(t) = u e^{-t}$ becomes\n\n$$\ny_2(t) = e^{-t}\\left( \\dfrac{c_1}{5} e^{5t} + c_2 \\right).\n$$\n\nWe can choose any values for $c_1$ and $c_2$ to yield a second solution. For example, if we set $c_1 = 5$ and $c_2 = 0,$ we get\n\n$$\ny_2(t) = e^{-t} \\cdot e^{5t} = e^{4t}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124287 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba3e0748-2a14-547e-b21e-c34251994a94" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Differential Equation Satisfied by the Reduction of Order Function"}] :question/id #uuid "bd03341b-1190-51fa-b3f0-6fd921e3430c" :question/math-academy-id "e-6574" :question/problem "The function $y_1(x) = e^{3x}$ is a solution to the equation $y'' - 6y' + 9y = 0.$ If $y_{2} = u(x) y_1(x)$ is a second solution of the equation, then which of the following equations must be satisfied by $u(x)?$\n\n- $u''=0$\n\n- $e^{3x}u'' + u=0$\n\n- $u''-u=0$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =u(x){y}_{1}(x) \\\\  & =u(x){e}^{3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & =u{e}^{3x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+3u{e}^{3x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{e}^{3x}+6{u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+9u{e}^{3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}-6{y}^{′}+9y & =0 \\\\ ({u}^{″}{e}^{3x}+6{u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+9u{e}^{3x})-6({u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+3u{e}^{3x})+9u{e}^{3x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{3x}({u}^{″}+6{u}^{′}+9u-6{u}^{′}-18u+9u) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{3x}{u}^{″} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $e^{3x} \\neq 0,$ we must have\n\n$$\nu'' = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388515 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba3e0748-2a14-547e-b21e-c34251994a94" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Differential Equation Satisfied by the Reduction of Order Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d86807e-183a-5f39-b04a-69f38669aac0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{″}-4xu=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388517} {:answer/id #uuid "f5348148-5daa-58d5-baef-6d4cdf4e84a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{″}={e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388518} {:answer/id #uuid "eae148de-88b5-5d9a-a3bf-bbb9b0d4cf6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{″}+4u=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388519} {:answer/id #uuid "e687c2d7-0fef-5849-989c-b5d7702bcfb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{″}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388520} {:answer/id #uuid "aca9bcca-4140-5f61-aeba-5f989e2759f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{″}+8u=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388521}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e687c2d7-0fef-5849-989c-b5d7702bcfb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{″}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388520} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6165270-94dd-57e1-ae78-90b0d82a5ca2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388516}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "02cdb037-5206-5f07-ac61-66ec161143d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-102791" :question/problem "The function ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{2x}$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}-4{y}^{′}+4y=0.$ If ${y}_{2}=u(x){y}_{1}(x)$ is a second solution of the equation, then which of the following equations must be satisfied by $u(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=u(x){y}_{1}(x)=u{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{2}=u{e}^{2x}$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{e}^{2x}+2u{e}^{2x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{e}^{2x}+4{u}^{′}{e}^{2x}+4u{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({u}^{″}{e}^{2x}+4{u}^{′}{e}^{2x}+4u{e}^{2x})-4({u}^{′}{e}^{2x}+2u{e}^{2x})+4u{e}^{2x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{2x}({u}^{″}+4{u}^{′}+4u-4{u}^{′}-8u+4u) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{2x}{u}^{″} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{2x}\\ne 0,$  we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388522 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba3e0748-2a14-547e-b21e-c34251994a94" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Differential Equation Satisfied by the Reduction of Order Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "609147b4-d37b-5941-aeaf-b564440f1641" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{u}^{″}+6{u}^{′}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388524} {:answer/id #uuid "2d7b656e-38e4-50b4-ba6b-a3d0673f1bda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{″}+6{u}^{′}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388525} {:answer/id #uuid "02d4702c-730c-5bc2-95b9-550efa8c5fcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{u}^{″}+3{u}^{′}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388526} {:answer/id #uuid "c4036936-efed-50ce-839e-0292b61fa685" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{″}+6x{u}^{′}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388527} {:answer/id #uuid "d8092722-f2fc-575c-a5c1-0f1593b8de41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}{u}^{″}+3{u}^{′}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388528}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "609147b4-d37b-5941-aeaf-b564440f1641" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{u}^{″}+6{u}^{′}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388524} :answer-field/id #uuid "24b524d8-8458-5dd4-aa56-df61559fe28d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388523}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e225d14b-6814-51a9-af43-900d2917ad01" :question/math-academy-id "q-102797" :question/problem "The function ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{3}$ is a solution to the equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-6y=0$ for $x>0.$ If ${y}_{2}=u(x){y}_{1}(x)$ is a second solution of the equation, then which of the following equations must be satisfied by $u(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=u(x){y}_{1}(x)=u{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{2}=u{x}^{3}$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{x}^{3}+3u{x}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{x}^{3}+6{u}^{′}{x}^{2}+6ux.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}({u}^{″}{x}^{3}+6{u}^{′}{x}^{2}+6ux)-6u{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{x}^{5}+6{u}^{′}{x}^{4}+6u{x}^{3}-6u{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{x}^{5}+6{u}^{′}{x}^{4} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{4}({u}^{″}x+6{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}^{4}\\ne 0$ for $x>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\nx{u}^{″}+6{u}^{′}=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125098 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7ae919f-4a7f-50e3-8c2f-7cea0b5a66ee" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Second Solution to a Differential Equation Using Reduction of Order"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c5fc7fc-2f90-556e-a8ba-841ee4345c94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{2}(x) = x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123611} {:answer/id #uuid "d996ab88-8a1c-5c7e-bf9f-eab5d48182f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{2}(x) = \\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123612} {:answer/id #uuid "1d4f5584-8fc7-5337-ab56-9e4c5e615f17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{2}(x) = e^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123613} {:answer/id #uuid "2c3ceaf6-e888-5fb4-82cc-4ead9996c36b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{2}(x) = x^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123614} {:answer/id #uuid "3a307f38-89b4-5782-9efc-cba840fb9d19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{2}(x) = \\frac{1}{x^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123615}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d996ab88-8a1c-5c7e-bf9f-eab5d48182f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{2}(x) = \\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123612} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a5512a1-ab4e-5f23-a650-68685dcdaff5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123610}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ed6836b6-963c-5315-9c37-229f40227bb4" :question/math-academy-id "q-64958" :question/problem "The function ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{3}$ is a solution to the equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}-3y=0$ for $x>0.$ Use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation." :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x)\\cdot {x}^{3}.$ \n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{2}=u(x){x}^{3}$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{x}^{3}+3u{x}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{x}^{3}+6{u}^{′}{x}^{2}+6ux.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}({u}^{″}{x}^{3}+6{u}^{′}{x}^{2}+6ux)-x({u}^{′}{x}^{3}+3u{x}^{2})-3u{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}({u}^{″}{x}^{2}+6{u}^{′}x+6u-{u}^{′}x-3u-3u) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{4}({u}^{″}x+5{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}^{4}\\ne 0$ for $x>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}x+5{u}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}x+5w=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}{x}^{-5}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}{x}^{-5}\\,⟹\\,u=-\\frac{1}{4}{c}_{1}{x}^{-4}+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u{x}^{3}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{3}(-\\frac{1}{4}{c}_{1}{x}^{-4}+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${c}_{1}=-4$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388529 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba3e0748-2a14-547e-b21e-c34251994a94" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Differential Equation Satisfied by the Reduction of Order Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44c6b3e9-b294-59c7-ba56-175d7c1bd6db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{″}-{u}^{′}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388531} {:answer/id #uuid "168b8500-96b4-502b-9131-120eb713112c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{″}+5{u}^{′}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388532} {:answer/id #uuid "a31c0423-1518-5b70-bfe4-964bfc0d580c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{u}^{″}+{u}^{′}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388533} {:answer/id #uuid "4554976a-f416-57b2-bb88-3603466b47ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{″}-{e}^{2t}{u}^{′}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388534} {:answer/id #uuid "bade2d21-9239-5ea7-9492-6137f2233202" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2t}{u}^{″}+{u}^{′}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388535}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "168b8500-96b4-502b-9131-120eb713112c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{″}+5{u}^{′}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388532} :answer-field/id #uuid "319d98fa-4154-59cf-a093-87baae3d32c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388530}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dce5b85d-ab16-573c-b856-3b30ff4fbb4b" :question/math-academy-id "q-64989" :question/problem "The function ${y}_{1}(t)={e}^{2t}$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-6y=0.$ If ${y}_{2}=u(t){y}_{1}(t)$ is a second solution of the equation, then which of the following equations must be satisfied by $u(t)?$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)=u(t){y}_{1}(t)=u{e}^{2t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{2}=u{e}^{2t}$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{e}^{2t}+2u{e}^{2t} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{e}^{2t}+4{u}^{′}{e}^{2t}+4u{e}^{2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({u}^{″}{e}^{2t}+4{u}^{′}{e}^{2t}+4u{e}^{2t})+({u}^{′}{e}^{2t}+2u{e}^{2t})-6u{e}^{2t} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{e}^{2t}+5{u}^{′}{e}^{2t} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{2t}({u}^{″}+5{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{2t}\\ne 0$ for all $t,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}+5{u}^{′}=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388536 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba3e0748-2a14-547e-b21e-c34251994a94" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Differential Equation Satisfied by the Reduction of Order Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1113c41-1136-5bf9-a861-a388bdc07556" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{″}-16{u}^{′}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388538} {:answer/id #uuid "cf54638f-8d75-5357-92c8-fc61691dc0c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{″}+8{u}^{′}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388539} {:answer/id #uuid "31558e27-fbf9-545d-a348-27349d2a7c64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{″}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388540} {:answer/id #uuid "ee38f740-c9fa-54e1-8d6c-92fc76611289" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{″}+2{u}^{′}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388541} {:answer/id #uuid "d2837b6b-8ef3-51f8-8195-2d8aaf263385" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{″}-4{u}^{′}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388542}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31558e27-fbf9-545d-a348-27349d2a7c64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{″}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388540} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b50acc6-7f2b-56d7-a93e-b953b5586d1e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388537}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e3f94cd0-0f26-5504-b218-e3f08dc30463" :question/math-academy-id "q-64993" :question/problem "The function ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-4x}$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+16y=0.$ If ${y}_{2}=u(x){y}_{1}(x)$ is a second solution of the equation, then which of the following equations must be satisfied by $u(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=u(x){y}_{1}(x)=u{e}^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{2}=u{e}^{-4x}$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{e}^{-4x}-4u{e}^{-4x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{e}^{-4x}-8{u}^{′}{e}^{-4x}+16u{e}^{-4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({u}^{″}{e}^{-4x}-8{u}^{′}{e}^{-4x}+16u{e}^{-4x})+8({u}^{′}{e}^{-4x}-4u{e}^{-4x})+16u{e}^{-4x} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{e}^{-4x} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{-4x}\\ne 0$ for all $x,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342544 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7ae919f-4a7f-50e3-8c2f-7cea0b5a66ee" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Second Solution to a Differential Equation Using Reduction of Order"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c49977a9-ac11-5940-8fd1-97d5fb0341de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{2}(x)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342546} {:answer/id #uuid "2c75840c-2d94-531b-8d9f-a96f1ae1a23d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342547} {:answer/id #uuid "cd102047-2d61-512e-918c-3838a762c61a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342548} {:answer/id #uuid "dbbfab88-5e2c-5129-8fc9-51fe7cbf9b27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342549} {:answer/id #uuid "7fe637e3-435a-5a7f-b032-f5a4f17c934a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{2}(x)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342550}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c75840c-2d94-531b-8d9f-a96f1ae1a23d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342547} :answer-field/id #uuid "d47a58ef-ea6d-5f72-bea3-4ba11429fa49" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342545}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "16ff2884-3d19-5ae0-a798-f85100f86ae1" :question/math-academy-id "q-64998" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}+9y=0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation." :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+3u\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-9u\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({u}^{″}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-9u\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)+9u\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″} & =-6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{cot}⁡3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}=-6w\\operatorname{cot}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}{csc}^{2}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}{csc}^{2}⁡3x\\,⟹\\,u=-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3}\\operatorname{cot}⁡3x+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x(-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3}\\operatorname{cot}⁡3x+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${c}_{1}=-3$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x(\\operatorname{cot}⁡3x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342551 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7ae919f-4a7f-50e3-8c2f-7cea0b5a66ee" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Second Solution to a Differential Equation Using Reduction of Order"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e718a58-cfbc-5efe-9414-0ef1ab930662" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{2}(x)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342553} {:answer/id #uuid "648e0682-b58c-53a8-a699-c7ea9ca86128" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342554} {:answer/id #uuid "54918d71-7b8c-52bf-ba39-c9e57a0e3dad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342555} {:answer/id #uuid "6e54b5a5-5e25-5c12-a450-5d9b1393593f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342556} {:answer/id #uuid "5086bfa6-3589-54df-96f2-d31a64efb773" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{2}(x)=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342557}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54918d71-7b8c-52bf-ba39-c9e57a0e3dad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342555} :answer-field/id #uuid "c327756f-014f-5e94-9e1c-2ef1f0a0dc03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342552}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2edeb67d-5fd1-5efb-89bb-3d7d3065db3b" :question/math-academy-id "q-65000" :question/problem "The function ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{2}$ is a solution to the equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-5x{y}^{′}+8y=0$ for $x>0.$ Use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation." :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x)\\cdot {x}^{2}.$ \n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{2}=u(x){x}^{2}$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{x}^{2}+2ux \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{x}^{2}+4{u}^{′}x+2u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}({u}^{″}{x}^{2}+4{u}^{′}x+2u)-5x({u}^{′}{x}^{2}+2ux)+8u{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}({u}^{″}{x}^{2}+4{u}^{′}x+2u-5{u}^{′}x-10u+8u) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}({u}^{″}{x}^{2}-{u}^{′}x) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}({u}^{″}x-{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}^{3}\\ne 0$ for $x>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}x-{u}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}x-w=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}x.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}x\\,⟹\\,u=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{2}{x}^{2}+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u{x}^{2}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{2}(\\frac{{c}_{1}}{2}{x}^{2}+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${c}_{1}=2$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{2}={x}^{4}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388543 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7ae919f-4a7f-50e3-8c2f-7cea0b5a66ee" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Second Solution to a Differential Equation Using Reduction of Order"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1192e38-4b82-53ef-81d5-c457ef9d7e29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{2}(t)=t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388545} {:answer/id #uuid "87505817-ee76-5620-878d-d7c7ab8c70fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{2}(t)={t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388546} {:answer/id #uuid "decf45e6-d8a6-5f93-a8a6-912aa1ed45f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{2}(t)=\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388547} {:answer/id #uuid "43a7e05e-5a83-5440-bb68-86b1720abc6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{2}(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388548} {:answer/id #uuid "0e6145c6-674d-53b1-8eb0-d035c8df6be3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{2}(t)=t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388549}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "87505817-ee76-5620-878d-d7c7ab8c70fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{2}(t)={t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388546} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1c61356-6674-5890-8451-bcb8eeadd725" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388544}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ede04736-3948-519e-a331-a0a63de50619" :question/math-academy-id "q-65020" :question/problem "The function ${y}_{1}(t)={t}^{2}$ is a solution to the equation ${t}^{2}{y}^{″}-3t{y}^{′}+4y=0$ for $t>0.$ Use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(t)$ to the equation." :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(t)\\cdot {t}^{2}.$ \n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{2}=u(t){t}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{t}^{2}+2ut \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{t}^{2}+4{u}^{′}t+2u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}({u}^{″}{t}^{2}+4{u}^{′}t+2u)-3t({u}^{′}{t}^{2}+2ut)+4u{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {t}^{2}({u}^{″}{t}^{2}+4{u}^{′}t+2u-3{u}^{′}t-6u+4u) & =0 \\\\ {t}^{2}({u}^{″}{t}^{2}+{u}^{′}t) & =0 \\\\ {t}^{3}({u}^{″}t+{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${t}^{3}\\ne 0$ for $t>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}t+{u}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}t+w=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{t}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{t}\\,⟹\\,u={c}_{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(t)=u{t}^{2}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)={t}^{2}({c}_{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)={t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388550 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7ae919f-4a7f-50e3-8c2f-7cea0b5a66ee" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Second Solution to a Differential Equation Using Reduction of Order"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62f2562e-0e39-5abb-b4fa-6070fcd0df6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{2}(t)={t}^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388552} {:answer/id #uuid "606a25e1-f61a-5e59-8daa-3fa1b4f2f40c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{2}(t)={t}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388553} {:answer/id #uuid "a1e85a2e-dbbb-55ab-902e-0152fc514e6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{2}(t)=\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388554} {:answer/id #uuid "e1ce4113-9fe9-5601-9be9-74bace2414f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{2}(t)=\\frac{{t}^{3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388555} {:answer/id #uuid "3fbb8f49-1f38-51dc-8abe-b0efb0c232ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{2}(t)=t{ln}^{2}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388556}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "606a25e1-f61a-5e59-8daa-3fa1b4f2f40c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{2}(t)={t}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388553} :answer-field/id #uuid "eca59f16-39e7-59f2-9d5c-6a352e80ddd1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388551}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0b96cb92-808f-528e-898e-4dcef68a7f9d" :question/math-academy-id "q-77666" :question/problem "The function ${y}_{1}(t)={t}^{3}$ is a solution to the equation ${t}^{2}{y}^{″}-5t{y}^{′}+9y=0$ for $t>0.$ Use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(t)$ to the equation." :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(t)\\cdot {t}^{3}.$ \n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{2}=u(t){t}^{3}$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{t}^{3}+3u{t}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{t}^{3}+6{u}^{′}{t}^{2}+6ut.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}({u}^{″}{t}^{3}+6{u}^{′}{t}^{2}+6ut)-5t({u}^{′}{t}^{3}+3u{t}^{2})+9u{t}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {t}^{3}({u}^{″}{t}^{2}+6{u}^{′}t+6u-5{u}^{′}t-15u+9u) & =0 \\\\ {t}^{3}({u}^{″}{t}^{2}+{u}^{′}t) & =0 \\\\ {t}^{4}({u}^{″}t+{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${t}^{4}\\ne 0$ for $t>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}t+{u}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}t+w=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{t}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{t}\\,⟹\\,u={c}_{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(t)=u{t}^{3}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)={t}^{3}({c}_{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)={t}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388557 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba3e0748-2a14-547e-b21e-c34251994a94" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Differential Equation Satisfied by the Reduction of Order Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "977b18b8-d6a2-5d14-acb7-f85494150aab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{″}+2{t}^{3}u=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388559} {:answer/id #uuid "ecacf528-0bf9-55d4-826f-1bca91b06409" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t{u}^{″}+3{u}^{′}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388560} {:answer/id #uuid "1d8e73bb-1fa2-5a2f-9098-5e7e2c6ce8d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{3}{u}^{″}-{u}^{′}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388561} {:answer/id #uuid "8e67f996-1188-5cfa-afe2-c456c6aa8664" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{u}^{″}-{t}^{3}u=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388562} {:answer/id #uuid "365b9b3b-99ff-5d37-bce5-5c8b652ae1b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{u}^{″}+t{u}^{′}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388563}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ecacf528-0bf9-55d4-826f-1bca91b06409" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t{u}^{″}+3{u}^{′}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388560} :answer-field/id #uuid "40fbb617-db41-5064-bb4f-1877a2022e9e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388558}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a9ab006c-953f-5a4b-abd9-63aa572d2d25" :question/math-academy-id "q-90524" :question/problem "The function ${y}_{1}(t)={t}^{3}$ is a solution to the equation ${t}^{2}{y}^{″}-3t{y}^{′}+3y=0$ for $t>0.$ If ${y}_{2}=u(t){y}_{1}(t)$ is a second solution of the equation, then which of the following equations must be satisfied by $u(t)?$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)=u(t){y}_{1}(t)=u{t}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{2}=u{t}^{3}$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{t}^{3}+3u{t}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{t}^{3}+6{u}^{′}{t}^{2}+6ut.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}({u}^{″}{t}^{3}+6{u}^{′}{t}^{2}+6ut)-3t({u}^{′}{t}^{3}+3u{t}^{2})+3u{t}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {t}^{3}({u}^{″}{t}^{2}+6{u}^{′}t+6u-3{u}^{′}t-9u+3u) & =0 \\\\ {t}^{3}({u}^{″}{t}^{2}+3{u}^{′}t) & =0 \\\\ {t}^{4}({u}^{″}t+3{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${t}^{4}\\ne 0$ for $t>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\nt{u}^{″}+3{u}^{′}=0.\n$$"}]}
